# The OSI and TCP/IP Models: How Layers Organize a Network

> Networking is split into layers, each serving the one above it and
> using the one below. This lesson maps the seven OSI layers, the four
> TCP/IP layers, and the TCP-versus-UDP choice that dominates practice.

## 1. Why Layering?

Chopping network functions into layers buys three things:

- **Modularity** — swap Wi-Fi for ethernet without touching browsers.
- **Interoperability** — any HTTP server works with any browser, on
  any operating system, over any physical medium.
- **Divide-and-conquer debugging** — a cable test probes layer 1,
  `ping` probes layer 3: failures get isolated fast.

## 2. The Seven OSI Layers

| # | Layer | Data unit (PDU) | Job | Examples |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 7 | Application | data | serves applications directly | HTTP, DNS, SMTP |
| 6 | Presentation | data | format, compress, encrypt | TLS, JPEG |
| 5 | Session | data | open, manage, close dialogs | RPC, NetBIOS |
| 4 | Transport | segment | end-to-end delivery, reliability | TCP, UDP |
| 3 | Network | packet | logical addressing, routing | IP, ICMP, routers |
| 2 | Data link | frame | one-hop delivery, MAC addresses | Ethernet, switches |
| 1 | Physical | bits | signals over the medium | cables, hubs, radio |

OSI is the *reference* model — a teaching and vocabulary framework.
The Internet actually runs the leaner TCP/IP stack.

## 3. Remembering the Stack

Bottom-up — **P**lease **D**o **N**ot **T**hrow **S**ausage **P**izza
**A**way — reads Physical, Data link, Network, Transport, Session,
Presentation, Application. Top-down the same stack reads **A**ll
**P**eople **S**eem **T**o **N**eed **D**ata **P**rocessing.

[tip]
Layer numbers are shorthand operators use: "a layer 2 switch" versus
"a layer 3 router" instantly tells you what the box can inspect.
[/tip]

## 4. Encapsulation and Decapsulation

Sending a message wraps it layer by layer; receiving unwraps in reverse:

@diagram tree id="encapsulation-layers" layout="tree" aria="Encapsulation adding a header at each layer down the stack"
  "Data (application)" -> "Segment (plus TCP header)"
  "Segment (plus TCP header)" -> "Packet (plus IP header)"
  "Packet (plus IP header)" -> "Frame (plus MAC header and trailer)"
  "Frame (plus MAC header and trailer)" -> "Bits on the wire"
@/diagram

Each layer treats the layer above's data as an opaque payload — the
**encapsulation** principle. Decapsulation strips the headers in
reverse order on the receiving machine.

## 5. The TCP/IP Model

| TCP/IP layer | Maps to OSI | Job | Protocols |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Application | 7, 6, 5 | everything user-facing | HTTP, DNS, SMTP, TLS |
| Transport | 4 | ports, reliability, flow control | TCP, UDP |
| Internet | 3 | IP addressing and routing | IP, ICMP |
| Link | 2, 1 | frames on the local medium | Ethernet, Wi-Fi |

## 6. TCP vs UDP

| Feature | TCP | UDP |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Connection | connection-oriented (handshake) | connectionless |
| Reliability | ACKs plus retransmission | none |
| Ordering | sequence numbers restore order | not guaranteed |
| Speed | slower | faster, minimal overhead |
| Header size | 20 bytes | 8 bytes |
| Data style | byte stream | discrete datagrams |
| Typical uses | web, email, file transfer | DNS, streaming, gaming, VoIP |

[example]
A web page (TCP) must arrive complete and in order — a re-sent packet
is better than a gap. A live voice call (UDP) would rather skip a
lost syllable than pause the conversation to request it again.
[/example]

## 7. Connections: The Three-Way Handshake

Before exchanging TCP data, client and server synchronize:

```text
Client                              Server
  | ---- SYN (seq = x) ------------> |
  | <--- SYN-ACK (seq = y, ack x+1) - |
  | ---- ACK (ack = y+1) ----------> |   connection open
```

Teardown uses FIN segments, acknowledged in each direction. UDP skips
all of this — send, and hope.

[summary]
OSI names seven layers with PDUs bits, frames, packets, segments, data;
TCP/IP compresses them into Application, Transport, Internet, Link.
Encapsulation adds one header per layer on the way down. TCP is
reliable and ordered, opened by the SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK handshake; UDP
is the fast, connectionless alternative for streaming, DNS, and games.
[/summary]
